
- 650 [[constituency]] - each has one [[MP]]
    - Means that a general election is really 650 smaller elections that then add up
    - Each constituency chooses an [[MP]]
    - 60,000-80,000 voters (roughly) per constituency
- Living in the U.K., Citizen, and 18 or older can vote
- 46 million registered voters in 2019
    - 32 million of these voted (Roughly)
- Candidate with the most votes in the constituency wins doesn’t need majority
- All 650 go to the house of commons and vote on laws
- 326 - Absolute majority
    - Working majority is less in practice due to Sinn Fein not taking their seats
    - 2017 - 322 for majority due to 6 Sinn Fein members not taking their seats
- 2017: Con - 314, Lab - 266, LD - 14, UKIP - 0, SNP - 34, OTH - 22 (Exit poll not the actual results)
    - Conservatives took supply and demand [[Coalition Government]] with DUP so they could form a majority government
 
- Most governments use [[proportional]] voting - half the vote share is half the government
- First-past-the-post - winner takes all system, name comes for horse racing,

- Pros:
- simple and familiar process
- 1 party government

- Cons:
- winning MP usually gets less than half of the vote - majority of people don’t want their MP, people can win as low as 24.5%,
- Smaller [[parties]] do not gain fair representation, UKIP in 2015 got 12.6% of the vote and only got one seat,
- Tactical voting becomes encouraged e.g. voting for Labour so conservatives don’t win even if you prefer another party. Voting for someone you don’t really like to keep someone out you really hate
 - 2/3 of constituencies are [[Safe seat]] - any vote that is not for the winning candidate is a wasted vote
        - 2011 voting referendum was voted down harshly
